[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings we have  more now in the conviction of delane  maxwell and what it could mean for  authorities trying to unravel jeffrey  epstein's sexual trafficking ring in  2018 miami herald investigative reporter  julie k brown uncovered new information  on epstein and maxwell's relationship  and activities she's the author of  perversion of justice the jeffrey  epstein story julie k brown joins us now  julie good to see you again good morning  good morning  so julie your reporting on jeffrey  epstein led to a larger investigation  and eventually brought us to yesterday's  guilty verdict you were in the courtroom  what were you thinking as you heard  the jury's verdicts  i think we were first of all we were a  little surprised because earlier in the  day that jury had indicated that they  still  uh were deliberating in earnest and in  fact they  brought up the topic of maybe  deliberating through the holiday weekend  they had also asked for some more  testimony so it was sort of sudden we  did not expect that it was going to  happen yesterday  and what was it like in the courtroom i  mean how did delane maxwell react what  was the audience reaction i know it was  probably full of reporters  it was full of reporters it was filled  with  u.s marshals who lined the walls behind  us it was  kind of ominous of what was to happen  the jury filed in they appeared very  tired uh you know they had been  deliberating uh 40 hours in total over  six days uh maxwell really didn't show  any reaction she she was she was shaken  as the verdict was read you could tell  because she almost kind of fell over she  wobbled a little bit and then when uh  you know the guilty verdicts were read  she just looked straight ahead and  really didn't show much emotion at all  finally sat down took a sip of water and  just slumped in her chair and  you know as i said she she just appeared  pretty stoic i think she was surprised  in so many ways this trial is about the  victims and julie you wrote for almost  15 years victims of jeffrey epstein  and galaine maxwell had been called  prostitutes had been lied to by  prosecutors had been viciously attacked  by defense lawyers and were traumatized  over and over by a system of justice  that catered to the wealthy and  politically connected couple who had  assaulted them as teenagers very  powerful statement there have you been  able to talk to any of the survivors  well i i spoke with a woman who had been  attending the trial almost every day  this this woman has been taking a  greyhound bus every morning from  philadelphia uh two hours in the morning  uh walking in the cold waiting in line  to get in  um it was  extraordinary and she sat and watched  this almost every day and she felt that  it gave her a sense of closure that she  could finally look uh maxwell in the eye  and feel like you don't you know you're  going to  you know you're not going to control my  life anymore i'm going to get past  julie speaking of those survivors um one  of the alleged survivors virginia dufres  she tweeted out maxwell did not act  alone others must be held accountable  where do things go from here  well she's absolutely right and uh she's  she's not alone in that feeling  epstein did not do this alone there were  many people that helped him he had a  whole ecosystem of people around him uh  the issue here is this case was a  challenge because these events happened  15 20 years ago  and you know memories fade the defense i  think  did a good job at poking holes in some  of the testimony because you know there  were dates that that weren't accurate  because you don't remember everything  exactly the same way when it happened so  long ago so this will pose a challenge  in any future  prosecutions  julie k brown thank you for being here  and thank you for your reporting

